1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:03,580
Today we use the number line to do operations.

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:06,448
Adding moves us to the right, subtracting moves us

3
00:00:06,448 --> 00:00:10,474
to the left, and multiplying makes equal jumps. Let's

4
00:00:10,474 --> 00:00:11,611
play a number game!

5
00:00:12,247 --> 00:00:15,598
Imagine a floor marked with numbers. A student stands

6
00:00:15,598 --> 00:00:19,057
on two. The teacher says: move three steps forward.

7
00:00:19,057 --> 00:00:22,922
One, two, three; she lands on five. So what

8
00:00:22,922 --> 00:00:26,857
did she just do? She added. Two plus three

9
00:00:26,857 --> 00:00:28,258
equals five.

10
00:00:28,877 --> 00:00:33,057
Let's remember our number line. Zero is the starting

11
00:00:33,057 --> 00:00:35,757
point. Then come one, two, three, four, five, and

12
00:00:35,757 --> 00:00:40,457
we keep going: six, seven, eight, nine, ten. The

13
00:00:40,457 --> 00:00:43,991
arrow shows that the whole numbers never end. And

14
00:00:43,991 --> 00:00:47,577
every step has the same size; that is what

15
00:00:47,577 --> 00:00:49,668
makes a number line useful.

16
00:00:50,333 --> 00:00:53,680
Now let's add on the number line. Two plus

17
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,460
three. We start at two. We make three jumps

18
00:00:57,460 --> 00:01:01,528
to the right: one, two, three. We land on

19
00:01:01,528 --> 00:01:06,456
five. So two plus three equals five. Addition means

20
00:01:06,456 --> 00:01:07,604
moving right.

21
00:01:08,207 --> 00:01:12,587
Subtraction is the opposite. Six minus two. We start

22
00:01:12,587 --> 00:01:16,567
at six. We make two jumps to the left:

23
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:19,913
one, two. We land on four, so six minus

24
00:01:19,913 --> 00:01:23,618
two equals four. Subtraction means moving left.

25
00:01:24,123 --> 00:01:29,289
Multiplication is repeated addition. Four times two. We start

26
00:01:29,289 --> 00:01:33,383
at zero and make four jumps, each of two

27
00:01:33,383 --> 00:01:37,763
units. Two, four, six, eight. Four times two is

28
00:01:37,763 --> 00:01:41,283
two plus two plus two plus two, which is

29
00:01:41,283 --> 00:01:43,634
eight. Multiplication means equal jumps.

30
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:48,913
Let's compare the three operations. Addition moves right: two

31
00:01:48,913 --> 00:01:52,420
plus three lands on five. Subtraction moves left: six

32
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:58,000
minus two lands on four. Multiplication repeats equal jumps:

33
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,291
four times two lands on eight.

34
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:04,130
Now a game from the textbook. Raju asks Gayatri:

35
00:02:04,130 --> 00:02:07,090
start at zero and jump three, then eight, then

36
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:11,290
five. Where will you land? First jump: zero plus

37
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:15,090
three is three. Second jump: three plus eight is

38
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:18,110
eleven. Third jump: eleven plus five is sixteen. So

39
00:02:18,110 --> 00:02:20,061
three plus eight plus five equals sixteen.

40
00:02:20,817 --> 00:02:24,437
Here is a simple routine for every number-line question.

41
00:02:24,437 --> 00:02:27,657
First, find the starting number. Then read the operation.

42
00:02:27,657 --> 00:02:30,237
Then decide how to move: right, left, or equal

43
00:02:30,237 --> 00:02:33,668
jumps. And finally, write down where you landed.

44
00:02:34,193 --> 00:02:38,233
Your turn! Here is the question: five times three.

45
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:40,127
How many jumps should we make, and how big

46
00:02:40,127 --> 00:02:43,187
is each jump? Start from zero, and pause the

47
00:02:43,187 --> 00:02:44,724
video to try it.

48
00:02:49,793 --> 00:02:53,413
Let's check. Five jumps of three units each. Three,

49
00:02:53,413 --> 00:02:58,556
six, nine, twelve, fifteen. So five times three equals

50
00:02:58,556 --> 00:03:03,184
fifteen: five jumps, and the landing position is fifteen.

51
00:03:03,810 --> 00:03:07,633
Let's recall. Addition moves right: two plus three equals

52
00:03:07,633 --> 00:03:11,798
five. Subtraction moves left: six minus two equals four.

53
00:03:11,798 --> 00:03:17,166
Multiplication makes equal jumps: four times two equals eight.

54
00:03:17,166 --> 00:03:20,930
And five times three equals fifteen. A number line

55
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:25,641
makes operations visible. Thank you for watching!
